  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1982-p17.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(t=3^x\\gt0.\\) Then \\(9t^2-28t+3=0,\\) whose roots are \\(t=3\\) and \\(t=\\frac{1}{9}.\\) Both are positive and each corresponds to one real \\(x,\\) so there are \\(2\\) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Substitute \\(t=3^x,\\) noting that \\(t\\gt0\\)",
        "The equation becomes a quadratic in \\(t\\)"
      ],
